        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 1016,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Turns points or vectors by a rotation given as a quaternion: a direction in a sensor's own frame into the room's frame, a set of marker positions by a body segment's orientation, a model's points by the way it is facing.\n\nOne quaternion turns every point; one quaternion per point (as many rows as there are points) turns each by its own - every joint's offset by every joint's orientation.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.rotation_apply\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nvectors:\nThe points or vectors, one row of x y z each (or a single x y z). Every input is turned and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nquaternion:\nThe rotation as w x y z, or one row per vector. Held.\n\ninverse:\nAn option, under the node. On, the rotation is undone instead - from the room's frame back into the sensor's.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrotated:\nThe turned points, the same shape.\n\nQUATERNIONS:\nFour numbers w x y z, scalar first - the same order as the quaternion nodes. A turn of an angle about an axis is w = cos(angle / 2) and x y z = the axis times sin(angle / 2): no turn is 1 0 0 0; a quarter turn about z is 0.7071 0 0 0.7071. q and -q are the same turn.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 0 0 - pointing along x - turned a quarter turn about z points along y: 0 1 0. The second node has inverse on and turns it the other way: 0 -1 0. A metro re-sends the vector twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nrotate_position turns about one axis by an angle. scipy.align_vectors finds the rotation between two sets of points. quaternion_to_matrix gives the rotation as a matrix for np.matmul.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1000,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Turns points or vectors by a rotation given as a quaternion: a direction in a sensor's own frame into the room's frame, a set of marker positions by a body segment's orientation, a model's points by the way it is facing.\n\nOne quaternion turns every point; one quaternion per point (as many rows as there are points) turns each by its own - every joint's offset by every joint's orientation.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.rotation_apply\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nvectors:\nThe points or vectors, one row of x y z each (or a single x y z). Every input is turned and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nquaternion:\nThe rotation as w x y z, or one row per vector. Held.\n\ninverse:\nAn option, under the node. On, the rotation is undone instead - from the room's frame back into the sensor's.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrotated:\nThe turned points, the same shape.\n\nQUATERNIONS:\nFour numbers w x y z, scalar first - the same order as the quaternion nodes. A turn of an angle about an axis is w = cos(angle / 2) and x y z = the axis times sin(angle / 2): no turn is 1 0 0 0; a quarter turn about z is 0.7071 0 0 0.7071. q and -q are the same turn.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 0 0 - pointing along x - turned a quarter turn about z points along y: 0 1 0. The second node has inverse on and turns it the other way: 0 -1 0. A metro re-sends the vector twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nrotate_position turns about one axis by an angle. scipy.align_vectors finds the rotation between two sets of points. quaternion_to_matrix gives the rotation as a matrix for np.matmul."
        },
